  "Number": 24,
  "Question": "Let \\(y=(x-a)^2+(x-b)^2,\\) \\(a,\\) \\(b\\) constants. For what value of \\(x\\) is \\(y\\) a minimum?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(a+b\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt{ab}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{a^2+b^2}{2}}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2ab}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Completing the square separates the part that changes with \\(x\\) from a constant. Expanding and regrouping gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=2x^2-2(a+b)x+a^2+b^2\\\\\n&=2\\left(x-\\frac{a+b}{2}\\right)^2+\\frac{(a-b)^2}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor real \\(x,a,b\\), the squared term is nonnegative, and the remaining term is constant. Thus the minimum value is \\(\\frac{(a-b)^2}{2}\\), attained exactly when the squared term is zero. Therefore \\(x=\\frac{a+b}{2}\\), choice A.",
